1
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:05,630
If you ever upgrade a Kubernetes cluster manually, you know the feeling.

2
00:00:05,769 --> 00:00:08,350
You hit Upgrade and then you cross your fingers,

3
00:00:08,689 --> 00:00:11,210
hoping your workloads survive the process.

4
00:00:11,329 --> 00:00:15,489
The good news is on GKE, upgrades don't have to feel like a gamble.

5
00:00:15,689 --> 00:00:19,389
Google gives you a built‑in mechanism to upgrade safely,

6
00:00:19,600 --> 00:00:22,239
predictably, and with minimal disruption.

7
00:00:22,420 --> 00:00:23,579
Let's see how we can do that.

8
00:00:23,780 --> 00:00:25,920
Google recommends using release channel,

9
00:00:26,299 --> 00:00:31,409
not these static versions, because this provides the predictable upgrades,

10
00:00:31,700 --> 00:00:37,290
reduce the surprise version jumps, and it simplifies the fleetwide maintenance.

11
00:00:37,619 --> 00:00:38,740
Now how you can do that,

12
00:00:38,860 --> 00:00:42,159
so for that you can run a simple command saying

13
00:00:42,439 --> 00:00:45,000
gcloud container clusters update.

14
00:00:45,299 --> 00:00:46,830
You give the name of your cluster.

15
00:00:46,939 --> 00:00:49,340
In this case, my cluster name is dev‑cluster.

16
00:00:49,729 --> 00:00:53,810
And I'm saying that release channel is regular and say Enter.

17
00:00:54,090 --> 00:00:56,549
Now this will update my dev cluster.

18
00:00:56,860 --> 00:01:00,009
If I want to see whether this has been done successfully

19
00:01:00,009 --> 00:01:02,810
or not, I can also go to the UI part,

20
00:01:02,900 --> 00:01:09,769
and there I can go inside the Kubernetes cluster, go to my specific cluster,

21
00:01:10,129 --> 00:01:13,779
that is a dev‑cluster, and inside that dev‑cluster,

22
00:01:14,169 --> 00:01:15,849
I can go down to the Details.

23
00:01:16,720 --> 00:01:20,440
And within the Details, I can see that the release tunnel

24
00:01:20,440 --> 00:01:22,919
which is mentioned here is a regular channel.

25
00:01:22,970 --> 00:01:27,040
Now on similar lines, when you do the upgrading of the nodes,

26
00:01:27,139 --> 00:01:29,800
it is recommended that you should have a port

27
00:01:29,800 --> 00:01:32,160
disruption budget which you can set it.

28
00:01:32,319 --> 00:01:34,440
Just go to the Open editor.

29
00:01:34,760 --> 00:01:39,940
In the editor, I have a file called payments‑pbd.yaml file.

30
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:45,120
Now in this file, I have set that the minimum availability is 2.

31
00:01:45,319 --> 00:01:50,099
Now this ensures at least two ports stay up during the node drain.

32
00:01:50,319 --> 00:01:54,180
Google strongly recommends PDBs before any maintenance.

33
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:55,790
Now how I can apply that,

34
00:01:55,800 --> 00:01:59,040
I can simply go down to the terminal and I can see here

35
00:02:00,099 --> 00:02:05,030
kubectl apply ‑f payments‑pbd.yaml file.

36
00:02:05,790 --> 00:02:08,229
Now this will create the policy for me.

37
00:02:08,380 --> 00:02:10,559
We also have search upgrades.

38
00:02:11,029 --> 00:02:13,259
That makes the GKE beautiful.

39
00:02:13,458 --> 00:02:16,149
Instead of draining a node, deleting it,

40
00:02:16,268 --> 00:02:18,869
recreating it, and rescheduling the pods,

41
00:02:18,979 --> 00:02:21,988
which can cause a lot of downtime, in GKE,

42
00:02:22,108 --> 00:02:25,328
you can add the temporary node first, shift the traffic,

43
00:02:25,548 --> 00:02:27,078
and then remove the old ones.

44
00:02:27,229 --> 00:02:30,619
This enables the zero downtime upgrades.

45
00:02:30,869 --> 00:02:33,869
How you can do that, that is also very easy to do.

46
00:02:34,149 --> 00:02:39,389
You can just say gcloud container node‑pools update default‑pool,

47
00:02:39,559 --> 00:02:43,470
give your cluster name and say max‑surge‑upgrade to

48
00:02:43,470 --> 00:02:46,300
1 and maximum unavailable to 0.

49
00:02:46,429 --> 00:02:49,899
It will add one extra temporary node during the upgrade.

50
00:02:50,149 --> 00:02:52,350
Now you can see that update has been done.

51
00:02:52,589 --> 00:02:55,320
You can also add some of the best practices,

52
00:02:55,330 --> 00:02:59,070
like you can use the maintenance window where you can

53
00:02:59,070 --> 00:03:01,979
restrict the upgrades to the predictable times.

54
00:03:02,190 --> 00:03:02,690
For example,

55
00:03:02,949 --> 00:03:07,130
you can simply use this command where you are setting the maintenance window

56
00:03:07,130 --> 00:03:11,009
start time and the maintenance window end time and say Enter.

57
00:03:11,149 --> 00:03:15,309
So this will restrict the upgrade to a specific time which you have given.

58
00:03:15,669 --> 00:03:18,070
Similarly, do the autorepair enable.

59
00:03:18,139 --> 00:03:22,559
In that case, GKE will automatically fix the unhealthy nodes.

60
00:03:22,789 --> 00:03:25,820
You can just say gcloud container node‑pools update

61
00:03:25,820 --> 00:03:28,429
default‑pool and enable‑autorepair.

62
00:03:28,610 --> 00:03:30,860
Seems my region was not right,

63
00:03:31,149 --> 00:03:33,820
so I just change the region and then it should work fine.

64
00:03:34,080 --> 00:03:34,850
Similarly,

65
00:03:34,990 --> 00:03:37,630
you can also let Google handle the patching

66
00:03:37,630 --> 00:03:41,100
automatically by auto‑upgrading enable.

67
00:03:41,259 --> 00:03:45,029
This is exactly how large scale production Kubernetes environment

68
00:03:45,029 --> 00:03:48,550
keeps the things running smoothly without downtime.
